---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'adoption' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Adoption: assist → warn → enforce"
  description="Assist and warn are available today through the extension. See how teams roll the convention out gradually, what changes at each stage, and where enforce — still on the roadmap — fits in.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / adoption</p>
          <h1>Adoption: assist → warn → enforce</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">The safest way to bring a convention to a team is gradual: help first, then make progress visible, then decide together whether — and how — to hold it firmly. Assist and warn work today through the extension alone. Enforce, as an authoritative, platform-level requirement, is still on the roadmap — see <a href={href('server', 'en')}>what's coming</a> for that part.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The rollout sequence</h2>
          <div class="timeline">
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--fg-faint)"><span class="wk">week 0–2</span><span class="md">assist</span><span class="ds">Install the extension and let people use it. No status is published anywhere. Nothing is measured beyond what each reviewer sees in their own browser.</span></div>
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--warn)"><span class="wk">week 2–6</span><span class="md">warn</span><span class="ds">Unresolved blocking points become visible directly on the pull request, without anyone being rejected. You can now talk about compliance without changing anyone's day-to-day workflow.</span></div>
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--block)"><span class="wk">later</span><span class="md">enforce</span><span class="ds">Not available yet as an authoritative, platform-level requirement. Track the roadmap for GitHub, Azure DevOps, and other platforms.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What the extension does</th><th>Effect on the pull request</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m">off</td><td class="d">Does nothing.</td><td class="d">None.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">assist</td><td class="d">Helps reviewers write compliant comments and reports diagnostics. Never blocks.</td><td class="d">None.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="color:var(--warn)">warn</td><td class="d">Same as <code>assist</code>, plus a visible count of unresolved blocking threads.</td><td class="d">Visible, never blocking.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">enforce</td><td class="d">Can refuse to submit a malformed comment locally, in the browser.</td><td class="d">No authoritative merge gate yet — that part isn't built.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Because nothing here depends on a server, moving from one stage to the next is a configuration change your team can make and reverse on its own — there's no separate infrastructure to bring up or tear down.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>One prerequisite worth setting early</h2>
          <p><strong>Configure <code>resolverOverrideGroup</code></strong> before relying on <code>decision</code> replies to close out blocking threads. This defines who may record a <code>decision</code> when the original author of a blocking comment is unavailable. Left empty, nobody can use that escape hatch, and a blocking thread can get stuck with no way to close it.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Rolling back</h2>
          <p>Every stage here is a single <code>mode</code> change in your configuration, applied through the same three-level system described in <a href={href('configure', 'en')}>configuration</a>. Moving back from <code>warn</code> to <code>assist</code>, or from <code>enforce</code>'s local strict mode to <code>warn</code>, takes effect the next time the extension reads the configuration — there's no separate service to restart or branch-protection setting to unwind, because none of these stages touch your platform's own merge rules yet.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>What to watch before going further</h2>
          <p>There's no organization-wide dashboard today — that kind of aggregate reporting is part of what an authoritative, platform-level check would eventually enable. In the meantime, what's worth watching is qualitative: are reviewers finding the right labels, are blocking threads getting resolved instead of stalling, and does the <code>warn</code>-stage count on a pull request usually reach zero before it merges. Uneven label use or blocking counts that never quite hit zero are both signals to slow down rather than push toward stricter enforcement.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Configuration for each phase</h2>
          <p>Only one setting needs to change as you move from one phase to the next: <code>mode</code>. Everything else should already be in place.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-k">&#123;</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"version"</span>: <span class="tk-v">1</span>,
  <span class="tk-k">"mode"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"warn"</span>,                    <span class="tk-cm">// assist → warn → enforce</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"resolverOverrideGroup"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"acme/tech-leads"</span>],
  <span class="tk-k">"formatSeverity"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"warn"</span>,          <span class="tk-cm">// formatting issues do not block</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"exemptUsers"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"dependabot[bot]"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"github-actions[bot]"</span>],
  <span class="tk-k">"toolCommands"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"/*"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@dependabot"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@renovate"</span>]
<span class="tk-k">&#125;</span></pre>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">worth checking before you enable it</span>
            <p style="margin:0"><code>toolCommands</code> is <strong>empty by default</strong>. In <code>enforce</code>'s local strict mode, a bare <code>/rebase</code> is rejected like any other non-compliant comment unless it has been declared. Adding <code>"/*"</code> exempts all slash commands.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="pager">
          <a class="pgl" href={href('labels', 'en')}><span class="dir">← previous</span><span class="ttl">Labels &amp; decorations</span></a>
          <a class="pgl" href={href('server', 'en')} style="text-align:right"><span class="dir">next →</span><span class="ttl">Enforce (roadmap)</span></a>
        </div>
</Docs>
